# Density estimates: geom_density

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])

alias Giggly.Plot

diamonds = Giggly.Datasets.diamonds()

render = fn plot ->
  plot
  |> Giggly.to_svg()
  |> IO.iodata_to_binary()
  |> Kino.Image.new(:svg)
end
```

## About this notebook

This notebook follows the examples on ggplot2's reference page for
[`geom_density()`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/geom_density.html), which covers
`stat_density()` too, in the order the page gives them. Each example shows ggplot2's code first and
then Giggly's. An example Giggly cannot draw yet keeps its ggplot2 code and names what it is
waiting for, so the page is also a list of what remains.

`geom_density()` draws a kernel density estimate, a smoothed version of the histogram, which suits
continuous data drawn from a smooth distribution; `stat_density()` computes it. Besides the
density, the stat computes counts, the density times the number of rows, for stacking densities.
Giggly has both, `Giggly.Plot.geom_density/3` and `Giggly.Plot.stat_density/3`, which take
ggplot2's options as keyword options (`bw:`, `adjust:`, `kernel:`, `n:`, `trim:`, `bounds:` and
`orientation:`), stack curves with `position: :stack`, and map what the stat computes, such as the
count, with `{:after_stat, [:count]}`. It has no `position_fill()`, and of the bandwidth rules `bw`
names, only R's `"nrd0"` and `"nrd"`, as `:nrd0` and `:nrd`, not `"ucv"`, `"bcv"` or `"SJ"`. A plot
is described with `Giggly.Plot` and drawn with `Giggly.to_svg/2`, which returns an SVG document.
The setup cell aliases `Giggly.Plot`, so the cells write `Plot.new/2`, and defines `render`, which
draws a plot and shows it below the cell with `Kino.Image`. Every example uses ggplot2's
`diamonds`, `Giggly.Datasets.diamonds/0`, which the setup cell binds to `diamonds`, ggplot2's name
for it.

## A density curve

```r
ggplot(diamonds, aes(carat)) +
  geom_density()
```

```elixir
diamonds
|> Plot.new(%{x: :carat})
|> Plot.geom_density()
|> render.()
```

## Horizontal densities

```r
# Map the values to y to flip the orientation
ggplot(diamonds, aes(y = carat)) +
  geom_density()
```

```elixir
diamonds
|> Plot.new(%{y: :carat})
|> Plot.geom_density()
|> render.()
```

## Rougher and smoother curves

```r
ggplot(diamonds, aes(carat)) +
  geom_density(adjust = 1/5)
ggplot(diamonds, aes(carat)) +
  geom_density(adjust = 5)
```

`adjust` multiplies the bandwidth the density is smoothed over.

```elixir
diamonds
|> Plot.new(%{x: :carat})
|> Plot.geom_density(adjust: 1 / 5)
|> render.()
```

```elixir
diamonds
|> Plot.new(%{x: :carat})
|> Plot.geom_density(adjust: 5)
|> render.()
```

## A curve for each cut

```r
ggplot(diamonds, aes(depth, colour = cut)) +
  geom_density() +
  xlim(55, 70)
ggplot(diamonds, aes(depth, fill = cut, colour = cut)) +
  geom_density(alpha = 0.1) +
  xlim(55, 70)
```

The limits drop the diamonds whose depth lies outside them before each curve is estimated.

```elixir
diamonds
|> Plot.new(%{x: :depth, colour: :cut})
|> Plot.geom_density()
|> Plot.xlim(55, 70)
|> render.()
```

```elixir
diamonds
|> Plot.new(%{x: :depth, fill: :cut, colour: :cut})
|> Plot.geom_density(alpha: 0.1)
|> Plot.xlim(55, 70)
|> render.()
```

## Bounded data

```r
# Use `bounds` to adjust computation for known data limits
big_diamonds <- diamonds[diamonds$carat >= 1, ]
ggplot(big_diamonds, aes(carat)) +
  geom_density(color = 'red') +
  geom_density(bounds = c(1, Inf), color = 'blue')
```

The diamonds of a carat or more have no values below 1, which the red curve, knowing no bounds,
smooths past. The cell keeps the rows R's indexing keeps, each column's values where `carat` is 1
or more, and writes R's `Inf` as `:infinity`.

```elixir
kept = Enum.map(Keyword.fetch!(diamonds, :carat), fn carat -> carat >= 1 end)

big_diamonds =
  Enum.map(diamonds, fn {name, values} ->
    rows =
      values
      |> Enum.zip(kept)
      |> Enum.filter(fn {_value, keep} -> keep end)
      |> Enum.map(fn {value, _keep} -> value end)

    {name, rows}
  end)

big_diamonds
|> Plot.new(%{x: :carat})
|> Plot.geom_density(colour: "red")
|> Plot.geom_density(bounds: {1, :infinity}, colour: "blue")
|> render.()
```

## Stacked densities

```r
# Stacked density plots: if you want to create a stacked density plot, you
# probably want to 'count' (density * n) variable instead of the default
# density
# Loses marginal densities
ggplot(diamonds, aes(carat, fill = cut)) +
  geom_density(position = "stack")
# Preserves marginal densities
ggplot(diamonds, aes(carat, after_stat(count), fill = cut)) +
  geom_density(position = "stack")
```

```elixir
diamonds
|> Plot.new(%{x: :carat, fill: :cut})
|> Plot.geom_density(position: :stack)
|> render.()
```

```elixir
diamonds
|> Plot.new(%{x: :carat, y: {:after_stat, [:count]}, fill: :cut})
|> Plot.geom_density(position: :stack)
|> render.()
```

## Conditional densities

```r
# You can use position="fill" to produce a conditional density estimate
ggplot(diamonds, aes(carat, after_stat(count), fill = cut)) +
  geom_density(position = "fill")
```

Not in Giggly yet: `position_fill()`.